      },
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": ", a right-wing group supporting numerous measures on Colorado’s 2026 ballot.",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "paragraph",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Short Description:",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "extended-heading",
    "version": 1,
    "tag": "h2"
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Establishes a constitutional right for consumers to purchase natural gas for use in homes and businesses and a right for producers, distributors, and utilities to sell natural gas, subject to the measure's terms.",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "paragraph",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Analysis and Commentary:",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "extended-heading",
    "version": 1,
    "tag": "h2"
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Oppose. Amendment 82 was placed on the ballot through a citizen initiative backed by operatives of ",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "detail": 0,
            "format": 8,
            "mode": "normal",
            "style": "",
            "text": "Advance Colorado",
            "type": "extended-text",
            "version": 1
          }
        ],
        "direction": "ltr",
        "format": "",
        "indent": 0,
        "type": "link",
        "version": 1,
        "rel": null,
        "target": null,
        "title": null,
        "url": "https://whoisadvancecolorado.com/"
      },
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": ", a right-wing group supporting numerous measures on Colorado’s 2026 ballot.",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "paragraph",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Amendment 82 is simple enough: putting a requirement that Coloradans use a particular energy source into the state constitution. Why should we require the use of any fuel, material, or otherwise in the state constitution? We shouldn’t.",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "paragraph",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Currently, natural gas is and will continue to be freely available in Colorado to consumers for purchase and businesses to sell. There’s no imminent state law, or city/county code aiming to threaten that. However, we do have a number of forward-thinking plans addressing how we might use natural gas and other fossil fuels in the future. In late 2025, the Colorado Public Utilities Commission approved such a plan:",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "paragraph",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "The Public Utilities Commission approved a “Clean Heat Plan” that calls for the state’s natural gas utilities to cut emissions from delivering and burning natural gas by 41% in the next 10 years, and all natural gas emissions entirely by 2050. We talked to state regulators, clean energy officials and advocates, and consumer groups for answers to your most urgent questions.",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "extended-quote",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "detail": 0,
            "format": 8,
            "mode": "normal",
            "style": "",
            "text": "Colorado plans to phase out natural gas heating in homes, prompting many reader questions. We have answers.",
            "type": "extended-text",
            "version": 1
          }
        ],
        "direction": "ltr",
        "format": "",
        "indent": 0,
        "type": "link",
        "version": 1,
        "rel": null,
        "target": null,
        "title": null,
        "url": "https://coloradosun.com/2025/12/05/colorado-natural-gas-phaseout-reader-questions/"
      },
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": " — Colorado Sun",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "extended-quote",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "The reality is that with new advances in appliances, home batteries, and electrical power generation, moving to all-electric residential and commercial buildings will be cheaper, safer, and with fewer emissions. The plan recognizes that the state will need significant investment and subsidies to meet the goal. Even so, the 2050 target date does not propose any additional regulation on propane tanks or other natural gas uses. Within this plan, a full transition will take decades, which is why it’s important not to undermine such efforts by putting a mandate to use natural gas in the state constitution.",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "paragraph",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Living in Boulder, Amendment 82 would overturn local ordinances that the city has implemented to speed the transition. Since December 2024, building code updates now ",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "detail": 0,
            "format": 8,
            "mode": "normal",
            "style": "",
            "text": "require all-electric construction",
            "type": "extended-text",
            "version": 1
          }
        ],
        "direction": "ltr",
        "format": "",
        "indent": 0,
        "type": "link",
        "version": 1,
        "rel": null,
        "target": null,
        "title": null,
        "url": "https://boulderreportinglab.org/2024/06/06/boulder-mandates-gas-free-all-electric-construction-for-new-builds-starting-december-2024/"
      },
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": " for new or substantially-retrofitted buildings. Cities need to move ahead of the curve on new construction because a building built today may last 50 or 100 years with energy infrastructure investments that will be obsolete by that time. Even if you are a fierce defender of natural gas usage, the ordinance allowed reasonable exceptions for “commercial buildings, including those with commercial kitchens and laboratories,” according to Boulder Reporting Lab.",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "paragraph",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Ultimately, there’s no good reason to require the use of natural gas in the state constitution. Our state and local governments need to be vigilant in moving ahead on an energy transition, and ensuring that every person and family in Colorado has the means to economically transition our energy supply. If Amendment 82 were to pass, that means we will undermine our own energy goals, add sunken infrastructure costs over the next several decades, and ultimately have to undo a measure that would be a significant mistake.",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "paragraph",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
